cartridge "NIGHT RAID/AHOY! FEB 1989/1989"

' NIGHT RAID
' A ColecoVision game based on "Night Raid", Ahoy! February 1989
' (Commodore 64 machine language, NIGHT RAID.ML at $C000, 2,692 bytes,
' started by a two-line BASIC loader; no author is named on the disk or in
' the program). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic),
' 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/nightraid.dis), the game is built for the
' ColecoVision: a starry night over a small town, hangar mouths on both
' sides of four flight lanes, smooth two-colour raider sprites, a
' crackling ray, explosions, sound effects and a title tune.
'
' The game: raiders fly out of the hangars, cross a lane, then come back
' out one lane lower, four lanes in all. A raider on the lowest lane lets
' down a ray that wrecks the first building (or the cannon) it passes over.
' Shoot them first with three guns: the centre cannon fires straight up,
' the left bunker fires up and to the right, the right bunker up and to
' the left. When the wave is over every building still standing is worth
' 1000; each 10,000 points rebuilds one; with nothing left standing at the
' end of a wave the game is over.
'
' From the original (orig/): four raider slots on four lanes (C64 Y 50,
' 86, 122, 158); a raider takes the next lane only when no other raider is
' on it, and waits at the hangar until it is free; 16 + (0-7) raiders a
' wave; eight raider kinds picked at random when one sets out, each with
' its side and speed (1, 2 or 3 pixels a move: 50, 100, 200 points); a
' raider on the lowest lane drops its ray (one building per pass, the ray
' then goes out); one shot at a time, from the cannon (stick centred +
' button: straight up, only while the cannon stands), the left bunker
' (left + button) or the right bunker (right + button), moving two pixels
' sideways for each one up; a hit is a box test (the C64 compared
' positions too); the bunkers can't be destroyed; after a wave a pause,
' the level up (15 at most), 1000 for every building left (cannon
' included), one rebuilt in order (cannon first) per wave in which the
' ten-thousands digit changed; game over at the end of a wave with all
' five gone and none to rebuild.
'
' Liberties: the C64 ran its loop flat out (about 33 passes a frame:
' a shot step every 8 passes, a raider step every 16 - level passes, so
' its level 15 moved raiders tens of pixels a frame); here everything is
' worked out per frame: the shot climbs 3.25 pixels a frame (the C64's
' 4 x 0.8 for the smaller screen), raiders 1.25, 2.5 or 3.75 pixels at
' level 1 and up to 2.56 times that from level 8 on (the C64 started
' about 40% faster and became unplayable); raiders come out of and go into
' hangar mouths drawn inside the screen (the C64's went off its edges); a
' raider waiting for a free lane waits in its hangar mouth; the ray checks
' both columns of a building (raiders move up to 8 pixels a frame); the
' explosion lasts 12 frames (8 on the C64); a high score; a wave-cleared
' message; the bonus is added as the wave ends (the C64 added it after
' its pause); the stars twinkle twice a second (the C64 rolled star bits
' every 8 passes); GAME OVER then the title (the C64 went back to PRESS
' FIRE BUTTON). The SHIFT pause key is gone (no keypad, no keys).
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): button alone fires the centre cannon,
' left + button the left bunker, right + button the right bunker. Holding
' the button fires again as soon as the last shot is gone (as on the C64).
' On the title, up and down choose, a button chooses.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` always given.
'
' Workarounds for Amy bugs jake-return-before-for removed 2026-11-01.

